{{!< default}}
{{!-- /digest-gen/ — admin-only Weekly Digest email composer.

     Single-page React tool (loaded via Babel-standalone) that lets
     admin users compose a Weekly Digest, pull essays from Ghost
     Content API, pull podcasts from Captivate (via the user-deployed
     mo-captivate-proxy worker), preview Free vs Paid versions, and
     export flat HTML for paste-into-Kit.

     Auth pattern matches the other /admin/* tools: shell renders for
     any signed-in member; the actual sensitive bits (Ghost Content
     API key, Captivate creds, Cloudflare Worker URL) are user-supplied
     and stored in the user's own browser localStorage. Non-staff who
     navigate here see the tool but can't pull anything until they
     paste their own keys, which only Ian holds.

     Source-of-truth for the tool lives in
     emails/weekly-digest-tool/ in the MOCA repo. Changes there should
     be mirrored here in assets/js/digest/. The two diverge only by
     the moDigestAsset() lookup added to email-template.jsx and the
     window-global lookup in app.jsx.

     Cloudflare Worker for Captivate proxy:
     emails/weekly-digest-tool/cloudflare-worker/captivate-proxy.js
     (deploy once; paste resulting .workers.dev URL into the Edit
     Content -> Pull Podcasts panel). --}}

<main class="admin-page">

  {{#if @member}}

    <section class="hero admin-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="hero-kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Admin &middot; Digest Generator</p>
        <h1 class="admin-headline"><em>Weekly&nbsp;Digest.</em></h1>
        <p class="admin-sub">Compose, preview, and export the Weekly Digest email. Pull essays from Ghost and podcasts from Captivate. Export flat HTML for Kit.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {{> "admin-nav"}}

    {{!-- Full-bleed: the digest tool's TopBar + Gmail chrome wants
         the whole viewport width. Skip the usual .container wrapper
         and let #mo-digest-root run edge to edge. --}}
    <section class="admin-body admin-body--digest" style="padding:0;">
      <div id="mo-digest-root" style="background:#f1e0c9;min-height:calc(100vh - 280px);"></div>
    </section>

    {{!-- Preconnect + load brand fonts the email template uses. The
         tool also needs them in the live preview, so we load them at
         the page level rather than inside the React tree. --}}
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IM+Fell+English:ital@0;1&family=IM+Fell+DW+Pica:ital@0;1&family=Source+Sans+3:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

    {{!-- Inject ABSOLUTE Ghost-hosted URLs for brand assets. The
         in-page <img> tags use these URLs directly — same-origin in
         the live preview, so they load normally — and because the
         URLs already start with https://, the export's rewriteImages
         step treats them as foreign and preserves them as-is. Kit
         recipients fetch them straight from mo-test.ghost.io (or
         mereorthodoxy.com after cutover; @site.url flips automatically).

         Why not the {{asset}} helper? It would output a relative path
         (/assets/images/X?v=hash) which would (a) get treated as
         "needs rewriting" by the export, (b) fail filename matching
         in any URL-override map because of the query string, and
         (c) end up data-URI-embedded — which Kit handles unreliably. --}}
    {{!-- Asset URLs that need the absolute @site.url for the digest
         tool's HTML export. Template can't externalize the JS object
         (paths contain Handlebars expansions), so emit as JSON in a
         meta tag and let digest-bootstrap.js parse it into
         window.MO_DIGEST_ASSETS. --}}
    <meta name="mo-digest-assets" content='{"mere-o-logo.png":"{{@site.url}}/assets/images/mere-o-logo.png","mere-fidelity.jpg":"{{@site.url}}/assets/images/mere-fidelity.jpg","christians-reading-classics.jpg":"{{@site.url}}/assets/images/christians-reading-classics.jpg"}' />

    {{!-- React + the digest tool's compiled JS modules. Order matters:
         tweaks-panel and email-template define globals (TweaksPanel,
         EmailTemplate, MO_TOKENS, DEFAULT_CONTENT) that content-editor
         and app reference.

         No Babel runtime: the JSX is compiled at deploy time via
         `npm run build:digest` (esbuild) and the .js outputs are
         committed alongside the .jsx sources. Removing Babel-standalone
         lets us drop 'unsafe-eval' from the theme's CSP. CI verifies
         the .js outputs are up to date with their .jsx sources.

         SRI hashes pin each CDN script to a known content hash. --}}
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/react@18.3.1/umd/react.production.min.js"
            integrity="sha384-DGyLxAyjq0f9SPpVevD6IgztCFlnMF6oW/XQGmfe+IsZ8TqEiDrcHkMLKI6fiB/Z"
            crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
    <script src="https://unpkg.com/react-dom@18.3.1/umd/react-dom.production.min.js"
            integrity="sha384-gTGxhz21lVGYNMcdJOyq01Edg0jhn/c22nsx0kyqP0TxaV5WVdsSH1fSDUf5YJj1"
            crossorigin="anonymous"></script>

    {{!-- Bootstrap: parses the mo-digest-assets meta into the global
         window.MO_DIGEST_ASSETS the React modules expect, and takes
         over the #root mount point. See digest-bootstrap.js. --}}
    <script src="{{asset "js/page/digest-bootstrap.js"}}"></script>

    <script src="{{asset "js/digest/tweaks-panel.js"}}"></script>
    <script src="{{asset "js/digest/email-template.js"}}"></script>
    <script src="{{asset "js/digest/content-editor.js"}}"></script>
    <script src="{{asset "js/digest/export-html.js"}}"></script>
    <script src="{{asset "js/digest/app.js"}}"></script>

  {{else}}
    {{> "admin-denied"}}
  {{/if}}

</main>
